{
  "id": "first-bad-version",
  "questionId": "first-bad-version",
  "slug": "first-bad-version",
  "title": "First Bad Version",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Binary search",
  "patterns": [
    "binary search"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "grind-75"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "good versions and bad versions each form one unbroken block, with all the bad ones after all the good ones - that's a sorted boolean condition",
    "checking versions one at a time from the start wastes checks - what if you jumped to the middle first",
    "if the middle version is bad, the first bad version is at mid or somewhere earlier; if it's good, the first bad version is somewhere later",
    "binary search on [1, n]: on each check, shrink the range toward the boundary between good and bad, and return that boundary once left and right meet"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "checking every version sequentially instead of binary searching, which defeats the point of minimizing checks",
    "off-by-one when narrowing the range, e.g. setting right = mid - 1 when mid was already found bad (losing a potential answer)",
    "not handling n = 1 or bad = 1 correctly if the loop assumes a range of at least two versions"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You have `n` versions of a product, numbered 1 to n, released in order.\nAt some point a bug was introduced, so every version from a certain\nversion onward is bad, and every version before it is good. You're given\n`n` and the first bad version directly as `bad`, standing in for a\nversion check you'd normally call one version at a time.\n\n**Find and return the first bad version, using as few version checks as possible.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= bad <= n <= 2^31 - 1",
      "every version >= bad is bad, every version < bad is good"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "the very first version is already bad",
      "only the very last version is bad",
      "n = 1 (single version)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 5, bad = 4",
        "output": "4",
        "expectedOutput": "4",
        "explanation": "versions 1-3 are good, versions 4-5 are bad, so the first bad one is 4"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 1, bad = 1",
        "output": "1",
        "expectedOutput": "1",
        "explanation": "the only version is already bad"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 10, bad = 10",
        "output": "10",
        "expectedOutput": "10",
        "explanation": "only the very last version is bad"
      }
    ],
